From e9c905cf01a19fd23a29245e6b5f57e5b76f6e69 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: leokbral <cabralleonardoferreira@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 09:45:56 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fix: break the ORCID placeholder-email dead end

ORCID accounts without a public e-mail are created with a deterministic
`<orcid>@orcid.placeholder` address. That address is undeliverable, and five
separate defects grew out of it.

- complete-profile: the form pre-filled the e-mail and posted it back, but the
  server rejects any non-empty `email` there (changing an address requires the
  verified flow). Every submit returned 400, and `required` blocked the only
  workaround of clearing the field, so the screen could not be submitted at
  all. The e-mail is now read-only and is not sent.

- orcid/callback: `isProfileComplete()` required a non-placeholder e-mail, so
  these accounts were redirected to /complete-profile on every login forever --
  a page that by design cannot change the e-mail. Completion is now signalled
  by `profileCompletedAt`, which complete-profile already wrote and nothing
  read. A real e-mail still counts, so legacy accounts that never visited the
  screen are unaffected.

- EmailEventDispatcher: `sendMail` had no try/catch inside the recipient loop,
  so one failing address aborted the loop and every later recipient of the same
  event silently got no e-mail. Failures are now recorded per recipient, and
  undeliverable addresses are skipped before SMTP is dialled.

- recovery: the endpoint generated and persisted a reset token before sending,
  leaving an unusable token behind and tripping the existing rate-limit in
  silence. Worse, the failure made the response differ from that of a
  non-existent account -- an enumeration oracle, and ORCID iDs are public.
  Placeholder accounts now get the same `success` response, with no token and
  no mail. The `catch` no longer returns `details` to the client; the full
  error stays in the server log.

- settings/account: the unusable placeholder was displayed as if it were a
  contact address. It is now hidden behind a persistent warning explaining that
  the account can only sign in through ORCID and that password recovery cannot
  reach it, pointing at the existing change-email flow.

The domain string lived in four places with three different semantics; it is
now a single helper, `$lib/helpers/orcidPlaceholderEmail`, which compares the
domain with `endsWith` rather than `includes` so a real address on a subdomain
is not misread as a placeholder.

