From 8c2aac1e876d383079b58d6c9b54c42dc14d3a2d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: leokbral <cabralleonardoferreira@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 14:52:55 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat: ask for an email instead of inventing one on ORCID
 sign-up

ORCID accounts whose record publishes no email address were created with an
invented `<orcid>@orcid.placeholder` address. That guess was the root of the
duplicate-account problem: the callback could not match the person to the
account they already had, so it made a second, orphan one -- and they could
never unify the two, because setting their real address on the new account was
refused with "This email is already in use" (the address belonged to their
first account).

Nothing is invented and nothing is created any more until the person proves an
address:

- The callback parks the ORCID identity in `orcidSignupClaims` and sends the
  browser to a form asking for an email. The browser carries only the claim id,
  in an httpOnly cookie; holding it is no privilege, since the confirmation
  link lands in the mailbox that was named.
- `POST /orcid/email` stores the address and mails a confirmation link. It
  deliberately never queries the user collection, so it cannot become a
  membership oracle for any address an attacker tries; the response is the same
  shape whoever owns it.
- `GET /orcid/confirm` is the proof of ownership ORCID could not give. If an
  account already holds the address, the ORCID iD is attached to THAT account
  -- nothing merged, nothing overwritten, the person simply gains a second way
  to sign in. Otherwise the account is created now, with that address, already
  verified. Either way the claim is consumed, so the link cannot be replayed.

Claims expire by themselves through a TTL index, so an abandoned sign-up leaves
nothing behind. The ORCID access and refresh tokens are deliberately NOT parked
in a claim: nothing in the codebase reads them (every projection excludes them),
so storing them there would be risk without purpose.

Accounts created by the old flow keep working. The callback still recognises a
legacy placeholder address for the ORCID iD and signs those accounts in; the
warning on /settings/account is what leads them to register a real address.

Account creation is now shared by both paths through
`$lib/server/orcid/createOrcidUser`, so username generation, the random
password and the verification fields cannot drift apart.

Consequence worth stating: because the schema requires a unique email on every
account, finishing an ORCID sign-up now requires giving an address. There is no
"continue without one" -- that option only existed because an address was being
invented.

